This is a classic IDOR / missing authorization-scoping bug — both routes trust `req.params.id` alone and never check it against the caller's `req.user.accountId` (set in `auth.js:11` from the session). Any authenticated user can edit/delete any account or cancel any order just by guessing IDs.

**`routes/accounts.js:6-18`** — `PATCH /:id` and `DELETE /:id` update the `accounts` table by `id` with no ownership check. Since a session only ever belongs to one account (`req.user.accountId`), the fix is to require that the target row's id matches the caller's account:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

Same pattern for `DELETE /:id`. (Since there's a 1:1 session→account relationship here, an even cleaner fix is to drop `:id` entirely and always operate on `req.user.accountId`, e.g. `PATCH /accounts/me` — that removes the possibility of this bug class recurring for this route.)

**`routes/orders.js:14-20`** — `PATCH /:id/cancel` updates `orders` by `id` only, but orders *do* have an `account_id` column (used correctly in the `POST /` insert at line 9). Scope the cancel the same way:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**General notes:**
- Always add the ownership predicate *inside* the `WHERE` clause of the write itself (not a separate `SELECT` check beforehand) — otherwise there's a TOCTOU gap between the check and the update.
- Return `404` (not `403`) when the row doesn't belong to the caller's account, so you don't leak whether the ID exists for someone else.
- Check `db.write`'s return shape — right now `res.json(row)` on `UPDATE ... RETURNING *` with zero matching rows will currently return `200` with an empty/undefined body instead of erroring, which is exactly what makes this bug silent. Any refactor should also handle that empty case explicitly, as shown above.
- Same audit is worth doing for any other `:id`-scoped route in this app (e.g. anything for order line items, addresses, etc.) since this looks like a repeated pattern rather than a one-off.

